      "content": "The gradient is $1{,}21\\text{ kg}^{-1}$ (using points $(1{,}2; 1{,}45)$ and origin $(0;0)$).\n$$\\text{Gradient} = \\frac{1{,}45 - 0}{1{,}2 - 0} = 1{,}21\\text{ kg}^{-1}$$\n\n**Final Answer:** $1{,}21\\text{ kg}^{-1}$\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\text{Gradient} = \\frac{\\Delta a}{\\Delta F_{\\text{net}}}$ (derived from Newton's Second Law where $F_{\\text{net}} = ma$, meaning $\\frac{a}{F_{\\text{net}}} = \\frac{1}{m}$)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\Delta a$ is the change in acceleration (in $\\text{m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-2}$)\n  * $\\Delta F_{\\text{net}}$ is the change in net force (in $\\text{N}$)\n* **Why we use it:** Since $F_{\\text{net}} = ma$, rearranging gives $\\frac{a}{F_{\\text{net}}} = \\frac{1}{m}$. Therefore, the gradient of an $a$ vs $F_{\\text{net}}$ graph represents the inverse of the mass $\\left(\\frac{1}{m}\\right)$, which gives units of $\\text{kg}^{-1}$!\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always include the correct SI unit for the gradient ($\\text{kg}^{-1}$) and remember that any valid pair of points on the line of best fit may be used for the calculation, though using points on the line rather than data points is preferred when a line is drawn."
