      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student correctly identifies $a=0$, but misses the frictional force acting on the tow truck tyres. I will provide the final worked solution, breakdown, and exam tip. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"0.45 * 1300 * 9.8\") -> 5733.0]",
      "content": "You correctly noted that acceleration is zero! However, the tow truck's tyres are in contact with the road, so we must account for kinetic friction acting on the tow truck as well.\n\nFirst, calculate the frictional force ($f_k$) on the tow truck using $f_k = \\mu_k N$ (where $N = mg$):\n$$f_k = (0{,}45)(1300)(9{,}8) = 5\\ 733\\text{ N}$$\n\nNow, applying Newton's First Law horizontally for the tow truck ($\sum F_x = ma_{\\text{net}}$ where $a_{\\text{net}} = 0$):\n$$F_{\\text{applied}} - f_k - T = ma$$\n$$9\\ 000 - 5\\ 733 - T = (1300)(0)$$\n$$9\\ 000 - 5\\ 733 - T = 0$$\n$$T = 3\\ 267\\text{ N}$$\n\n**Final Answer:** Tension $T = 3\\ 267\\text{ N}$\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\sum F_x = ma$ and $f_k = \\mu_k N$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $F_{\\text{applied}}$: The forward pulling force from the tow truck's engine ($9\\ 000\\text{ N}$)\n  * $f_k$: The frictional force resisting the tow truck's movement across the road ($5\\ 733\\text{ N}$)\n  * $T$: The tension force transmitted through the tow bar to the car\n* **Why we use it:** Newton's First Law tells us that when velocity is constant, all forces in the horizontal direction must balance out. The engine's forward force is shared between overcoming road friction and pulling the car via tension.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always isolate objects (draw separate free-body diagrams for the tow truck and the car) when dealing connected bodies. A common mistake is forgetting to include the friction acting on the tow truck itself."
    }
